name statement
# What a witness named at run time states (laplace witness): feedback on an answer, an attested fact, an outcome, a
# measured cost. A claim a line, its parts between tabs:
#   subject <tab> relation <tab> object <tab> score
# Each part is the text it is, the same entity wherever that text stands: a subject that is a recorded answer is that
# answer. The claim is [subject, relation, object], or [subject, relation] where no object is written. The score is the
# outcome, from 0 (it does not hold) to 1 (it holds), a draw halfway; none written is a win. The witness, its trust
# class and its lineage are the command's, never the file's.
match *.statement

tier row by \n
# A part that holds a line break, a tab or a backslash, a program's code or a message of many lines, writes it after a
# backslash: the part is the text it is, every character of it.
escaped \
names subject relation object score
tier field by \t

# A statement is its content alone: the name of the file a program wrote it to is no part of it, so the same lines
# are the same trunk wherever they were written.
omit pathname filename

thing row subject
content subject relation object
# the relation is the witness's own word for it, as written
value - relation
relate row relation to object alone
score row score from 0 to 1
# Read whole or refused: a line that attests nothing, or writes a score that is no number from 0 to 1, stops the run.
strict row
